WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:01.801
-Good evening.

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:02.834
I'm Seth Meyers.
This is "Late Night."

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:04.234
We hope you're
doing well tonight.

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:05.400
And now, if you don't mind,
we're gonna get to the news.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.000
President Biden signed
an executive order today

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.767
that increases background checks
on gun purchases.

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:12.534
From now on, gun dealers

00:00:12.534 --> 00:00:15.667
are gonna ask your first
and last name.

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:17.834
[ Laughter ]

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:19.734
Republican presidential hopeful
Nikki Haley

00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:21.501
said on Sunday that taxpayers

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:24.667
"should absolutely not bail out
Silicon Valley Bank."

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.534
But I think that's
largely because

00:00:26.534 --> 00:00:28.267
it's polling ahead of her.

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:30.300
[ Laughter ]

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.834
The Pentagon said today
that a Russian jet

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:36.200
crashed into a U.S. military
drone over the Black Sea.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.501
And I think I speak for
all Americans when I say,

00:00:38.501 --> 00:00:39.734
probably an accident, right?

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:43.000
This doesn't have to be
a whole thing.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.000
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.400
Just go with accident.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:49.300
Russia is reportedly
restoring hundreds

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.667
of decades-old Soviet tanks
to use against Ukraine

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:54.701
amid a shortage of weapons
and equipment.

00:00:54.701 --> 00:00:56.033
Oh, they are struggling.

00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:58.000
Next it's just gonna be,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.133
"Any way to trick out
these T-shirt cannons?"

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:03.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:04.934
Fox News host Brian Kilmeade

00:01:04.934 --> 00:01:07.467
interviewed Florida governor
Ron DeSantis over the weekend

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.400
and played catch
while wearing suits.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.734
It kind of ruined the flow
of the interview, though.

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:15.067
"So, Governor, what do you think
the U.S. role should be

00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:17.000
in the war in --
Oh, wait, car!"

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.133
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:25.133
That's right, Fox News aired
an interview over the weekend

00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:26.901
where host Brian Kilmeade
played catch

00:01:26.901 --> 00:01:28.434
with Florida governor
Ron DeSantis

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:30.367
at a high school baseball field
in Iowa.

00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:32.200
And no surprise here,
their fathers chose

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.033
not to come out of the corn
and reconnect with them.

00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:36.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:47.100
Democrats are reportedly worried
that efforts to ban TikTok

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.200
could make it harder
to reach young voters.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.968
Well, you could do
what Republicans do

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:52.467
and reach them on Venmo.

00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:55.133
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.434 --> 00:01:58.934
Just a thing that happened.

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:01.467
I don't know why everybody's
so weird about it.

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:03.434
Former president Trump
has reportedly been invited

00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:05.534
to testify before
a New York grand jury

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:07.100
as part of the Manhattan
district attorney's

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:08.934
investigation
into hush-money payments

00:02:08.934 --> 00:02:10.934
that were made to
adult film star Stormy Daniels.

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:13.434
And despite everything,
Trump was still excited

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:16.367
to get invited to something
in New York again.

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:19.033
[ As Trump ] I'm back, baby.

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:21.067
Any -- Any word
from the Met Ball?

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:23.501
Have they reached out?

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:26.067
[ Normal voice ] California
congressman Mark Takano

00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:27.567
recently reintroduced a bill

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:31.434
that would make a 32-hour
workweek a national standard,

00:02:31.434 --> 00:02:32.934
which would finally
give Americans

00:02:32.934 --> 00:02:35.634
the free time they need
to look for a third job.

00:02:35.634 --> 00:02:37.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.100
Microsoft is set on Thursday

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.734
to hold a "Future of Work with
AI" event, and this is scary --

00:02:42.734 --> 00:02:45.934
None of the executives
can remember organizing it.

00:02:45.934 --> 00:02:48.133
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:54.834
"Playboy" is reportedly planning

00:02:54.834 --> 00:02:57.367
to relaunch
as a digital-first magazine,

00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:01.634
and, dude, I just found
my dad's stash in his closet.

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:03.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.601 --> 00:03:13.033
"The New York Times"
published an op-ed today titled

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:15.267
"Working From Home Is
Less Healthy Than You Think."

00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:17.267
Oh, how unhealthy could it be?

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:20.267
I mean -- Oh!
Oh, crap.

00:03:20.267 --> 00:03:22.000
Is that a ghost?

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.267
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:26.801
The NYPD is looking
for a group of teens

00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:30.234
who allegedly stole more than
$40,000 worth of calculators

00:03:30.234 --> 00:03:31.501
from high schools in the Bronx.

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:33.801
Said the teens,
"Actually, it's...

00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:38.767
$41,672."

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.467
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.467 --> 00:03:42.067
That's how you do it, right?

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:49.000
In my -- In my --
In my mind's eye,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.467
it also had that little
paper thing that was...

00:03:57.834 --> 00:04:00.467
And fin-- Alright.
We're gonna go --

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:03.000
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.234
Oh, boy.

00:04:06.234 --> 00:04:08.601
How hopeful are you guys
based on this photo?

00:04:08.601 --> 00:04:10.767
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:17.033
And finally, a clam was recently
discovered in Florida

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:19.000
that's believed to be
more than 200 years old.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.300
And like most 200-year-old clams
in Florida,

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.801
it moved there from
the Upper West Side.

00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:26.067
You guys, we just --
That was the monologue.

00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:28.701
[ Cheers and applause ]
It's officially a monologue.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.300
We got a great show
for you tonight.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.133
He's an Emmy-winning actor and
the star of "The Blacklist,"

00:04:33.133 --> 00:04:35.534
which hits 200 episodes
this Sunday.

00:04:35.534 --> 00:04:38.801
The fantastic James Spader
will be here.

00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:41.501
Friend of the show James Spader.

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.801
You know him from "Deadwood"
and "American Gods."

00:04:43.801 --> 00:04:46.667
He's currently starring
in "John Wick: Chapter 4,"

00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:47.801
which is out next weekend.

00:04:47.801 --> 00:04:50.167
Ian McShane is joining us.

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:51.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:53.701
And she is a fantastic comedian.

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:55.367
She stars in "The Ms. Pat Show"

00:04:55.367 --> 00:04:59.234
and is kicking off her "Ya Girl
Done Made It" stand-up tour.

00:04:59.234 --> 00:05:02.267
Our friend Ms. Pat
will also be here.

00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:03.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:06.667
Before we get to all that,

00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:08.300
these days, the news stinks.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:09.634
I don't have to tell you that.

00:05:09.634 --> 00:05:11.968
Every headline I see makes me
feel worse than the last.

00:05:11.968 --> 00:05:15.367
Every once in a while, there's a
story that makes me feel great.

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:17.367
I'd like to share one
in a segment

00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:19.767
called "The Kind of Story
We Need Right Now."

00:05:19.767 --> 00:05:21.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:23.734 --> 00:05:26.300
This is a Eurasian eagle-owl,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.300
but it's not just any
Eurasian eagle-owl.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.067
This owl's name is Flaco,

00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:32.300
and last month he was
minding his own business,

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.000
living in the Central Park Zoo,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.868
when some vandals cut a hole
in his enclosure

00:05:36.868 --> 00:05:40.400
and Flaco did what any
self-respecting owl in captivity

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:42.067
would do -- he escaped.
That's right.

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:44.367
Flaco Shawshanked
out of that cage,

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:48.667
which according to one article
was the size of a bus stop

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:50.801
and which he'd been living in
for 12 years,

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:52.567
and he started flying
around Central Park.

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:54.968
This is the kind of story
we need right now.

00:05:54.968 --> 00:05:56.901
Every New Yorker dreams
of moving out

00:05:56.901 --> 00:06:01.267
of their tiny [bleep] apartment
into a nicer neighborhood,

00:06:01.267 --> 00:06:03.334
and Flaco did it.

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:05.667
Every New Yorker wants to live
on Central Park.

00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:08.400
Suddenly this dude was living
in Central Park.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.868
Without a trust fund.
Incredible.

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:14.100
Also, Flaco had been living
next to the penguin enclosure,

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.701
and if there's one thing
every New Yorker

00:06:15.701 --> 00:06:17.167
has had to put up with
at some point,

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:20.634
it's living next to somebody
who eats a ton of fish.

00:06:20.634 --> 00:06:24.334
My man Flaco
escaped a nightmare.

00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:26.200
Not long after Flaco
left the zoo,

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:27.701
he was spotted on Fifth Avenue

00:06:27.701 --> 00:06:30.100
peering in the window
at Bergdorf Goodman.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.133
Of course he was.

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:32.667
He spent 12 years
living in Manhattan.

00:06:32.667 --> 00:06:35.033
Never got to see the sights.

00:06:35.033 --> 00:06:37.601
That's why he was also spotted
at the Statue of Liberty,

00:06:37.601 --> 00:06:41.200
at the M&amp;M's store, and in
the front row at "Hamilton."

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.067
There he is.

00:06:43.067 --> 00:06:45.667
Probably a montage
of him somewhere

00:06:45.667 --> 00:06:47.267
trying on a bunch of outfits.

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:49.667
Flaco's escape created
an exciting opportunity

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:51.100
for him to spread his wings.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:53.234
But more importantly,
it created an opportunity

00:06:53.234 --> 00:06:55.834
for reporters to say owl puns.

00:06:55.834 --> 00:06:56.968
-It's a "Hoodunit."

00:06:56.968 --> 00:06:57.968
-"Hoodunit."

00:06:57.968 --> 00:07:00.300
-Hoo-hoo-hoo-dunit.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.200
It's a "how did you not

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:04.734
come up with something better
than that?"

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:06.801
I love that they don't even
come up with different puns.

00:07:06.801 --> 00:07:08.567
It's like they're all
using the same copy

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:10.100
of "Bartlett's Puns
for Reporters."

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.267
But the best part
of this whole story

00:07:12.267 --> 00:07:13.701
is everybody
underestimated Flaco.

00:07:13.701 --> 00:07:16.868
When he first escaped, an owl
expert told "The New York Times"

00:07:16.868 --> 00:07:18.634
that Flaco would be easy
to catch because,

00:07:18.634 --> 00:07:22.133
in his experience, owls
were not particularly smart.

00:07:22.133 --> 00:07:25.667
Oh, yeah? Then why are they
the only birds who graduate?

00:07:25.667 --> 00:07:27.834
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:29.067
Authorities were worried Flaco

00:07:29.067 --> 00:07:30.934
wouldn't be able to feed himself
in the wild

00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:32.834
because he had spent
his whole life in captivity.

00:07:32.834 --> 00:07:34.767
Well, guess what owls eat.

00:07:34.767 --> 00:07:35.968
Rodents and birds.

00:07:35.968 --> 00:07:38.667
Worrying an owl will starve
in Central Park

00:07:38.667 --> 00:07:40.467
is like worrying a toddler
will starve

00:07:40.467 --> 00:07:43.300
in a chicken nugget factory.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:46.467
Flaco did just fine on his own
because Flaco's not a dumbass,

00:07:46.467 --> 00:07:49.300
but zoo officials were still
determined to get him back.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.033
So they set up
an elaborate ruse.

00:07:51.033 --> 00:07:54.300
They left a dead rat
out in an open field,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.667
and they put it inside a trap,

00:07:56.667 --> 00:08:00.267
and then they played a recording
of owl mating calls

00:08:00.267 --> 00:08:02.901
to lure Flaco to it.

00:08:02.901 --> 00:08:04.100
And you know what happened next?

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:06.133
Flaco swooped down,
grabbed the rat,

00:08:06.133 --> 00:08:08.467
but didn't stay long enough
to get caught in the trap.

00:08:08.467 --> 00:08:10.334
But he did stay long enough

00:08:10.334 --> 00:08:15.033
to look directly at a camera
and pose.

00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:17.801
Look at his face.

00:08:17.801 --> 00:08:20.467
I don't know Flaco, but I'm
pretty sure that face is saying,

00:08:20.467 --> 00:08:22.400
"Do I look like
a [bleep] idiot?"

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.934
[ Laughter ]

00:08:24.934 --> 00:08:26.234
The zoo officials were like,

00:08:26.234 --> 00:08:28.133
"We shouldn't tell anybody
about this."

00:08:28.133 --> 00:08:29.667
[ Laughter ]

00:08:29.667 --> 00:08:31.667
They made several more attempts
to catch Flaco.

00:08:31.667 --> 00:08:33.701
They followed him around
Central Park with nets.

00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:35.934
The NYPD even tried to catch him
with a dog crate.

00:08:35.934 --> 00:08:37.200
Nothing worked.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.701
And pretty soon New Yorkers
started putting pressure

00:08:38.701 --> 00:08:40.334
on officials
to just leave Flaco alone.

00:08:40.334 --> 00:08:41.667
And after weeks of trying
to catch him,

00:08:41.667 --> 00:08:43.167
zoo officials did just that.

00:08:43.167 --> 00:08:45.300
They announced they would end
their attempts to capture Flaco

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:46.434
and let him be free.

00:08:46.434 --> 00:08:48.234
This is the kind of story
we need right now.

00:08:48.234 --> 00:08:51.234
Everyone underestimated Flaco.

00:08:51.234 --> 00:08:52.934
They said he couldn't survive
on his own.

00:08:52.934 --> 00:08:54.033
He did it.

00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:55.467
This is a story
for the underdogs,

00:08:55.467 --> 00:08:56.801
a story for anybody
who's ever been told,

00:08:56.801 --> 00:08:58.167
"You're too short
to play basketball,"

00:08:58.167 --> 00:08:59.434
or, "Girls can't do math,"

00:08:59.434 --> 00:09:01.834
or, "J.Lo is never gonna
date you a second time."

00:09:01.834 --> 00:09:04.434
So tonight, you walk
through Central Park,

00:09:04.434 --> 00:09:05.501
look up at the sky.

00:09:05.501 --> 00:09:06.901
You just might see
something amazing.

00:09:06.901 --> 00:09:09.901
You might see Flaco flying free
with a rat in one talon

00:09:09.901 --> 00:09:12.334
and a "Hamilton" playbill
in the other,

00:09:12.334 --> 00:09:15.801
'cause this is the kind of story
we need right now.

00:09:15.801 --> 00:09:17.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

